As far as I understand the issue a browser that accepts, e.g., an
"ftp:" URI must implement the ftp protocol (or delegate it to another
ftp client). What the browser then does is just displaying the files
and directories.

An intelligent client ("thick client") is an application on the users
side that knows about the protocol(s) he wants to handle *and*
to provide client side (and user specific) features; like Real
Newsreaders, Real Mailclients, etc. A http(s) supporting browser
is a "thin client" that (somewhat simplified) just displays pages
written in HTML (and/or rudimentary XML, if you like to mention).
The application logic, the "intelligence", is at the side of the web
application, at google in this case, where the page to display is
built. User specific application logic, like kill files, how to
memorize what articles have been read, etc., can usually only
be stored by mechanisms like cookies, if at all; and then security
and privacy concerns will step in, and a usability degradation, if
you want to clear that data...

But, IIRC, the excitement within this thread came from another
source, not about using this or that newsreader tool, but simply
about writing complete postings, with subject, complete body,
context, and in correct order; to make it possible to orderly reply.
